MANDATORY: Before completing ANY code change, perform Impact Analysis.
Every code change in this DDD/CQRS codebase has ripple effects. You MUST trace the full dependency chain and update ALL affected files. Never submit a partial change.
The Dependency Chain
A single change to a domain entity can affect up to 10+ files. Trace this chain for EVERY change:
Domain Entity changed?
  → Mapper (domain ↔ TypeORM)
  → TypeORM Entity (infrastructure/entities/)
  → Migration (if schema changed)
  → migrations.ts registry
  → Repository interface (domain/)
  → Repository implementation (infrastructure/repositories/)
  → Command/Query handlers that use this entity
  → Facade (abstract + real) if handler signatures changed
  → Controller + DTOs if facade signatures changed
  → Unit tests for handlers/mappers
  → E2E tests if API contract changed
  → Prompt templates if entity data is injected into AI prompts
Impact Analysis Checklist
After writing new code or modifying existing code, ALWAYS run through this checklist BEFORE presenting the result:
Layer 1: Domain Changes

 Domain entity modified? → Update the mapper, TypeORM entity, and any business rules
 New field on entity? → Add to: mapper (both directions), TypeORM entity column, migration, DTOs (request + response), repository queries if used in filters
 Field removed from entity? → Same chain as above, in reverse — remove from all layers
 New enum value? → Check all switch/if statements across handlers that use this enum
 Business rule changed? → Check all command handlers that call checkRule() with this rule

Layer 2: Infrastructure Changes

 TypeORM entity changed? → Generate migration, register in migrations.ts
 Repository method added/changed? → Update abstract interface in domain layer, update all callers in command/query handlers
 New relationship/FK? → Update cascade rules, check delete flows, update mappers for nested entities

Layer 3: Application Changes

 Command handler signature changed? → Update facade method (abstract + real), update controller, update DTOs
 Query handler return type changed? → Update facade, controller response DTO, any event handlers that call this query
 New domain event emitted? → Check if existing event handlers need updating, or create new handler
 Event handler modified? → Check if other modules listen to the same event

Layer 4: HTTP/API Changes

 Controller endpoint changed? → Update request/response DTOs, check mobile app API routes in d_* modules
 DTO field added/removed? → Verify class-validator decorators, check if mobile DTOs need updating
 Auth guard changed? → Check all routes using that guard

Layer 5: Cross-Module Dependencies

 Compass module changed? → Check: AiModule, PromptDomain (prompt templates may need updating)
 Note module changed? → Check: AiModule, PromptDomain (note-summarize.hbs)
 Account module changed? → Check: PurchaseDomain (entitlements), all modules using GetAccountById or GetIsPremiumAccount
 User Profile changed? → Check: Compass (belief system determines AI archetype), Quote (belief-based filtering), Calendar (belief-based events)
 Notification module changed? → Check: Path (reminders), Calendar (event alerts)
 Purchase module changed? → Check: Account (entitlements sync)
 Prompt templates changed? → Check: all command handlers that compile and use that template

Layer 6: AI Integration

 Prompt template modified? → Verify all Handlebars variables ({{var}}) still exist in the context passed by the query handler
 AI tool/function definition changed? → Update the handler that processes tool call responses
 AiFacade params changed? → Update all callers across Compass, Note, Quote, Calendar modules

Layer 7: Testing

 Any handler changed? → Update or create unit test (.spec.ts co-located)
 API contract changed? → Update E2E tests in test/
 AI behavior changed? → Update AiFakeFacade mock expectations in tests

Cross-Repository Awareness
This backend serves the Flutter mobile app. Flag (do not silently ignore) when changes affect the mobile API contract:

New/removed API fields → Mobile DTOs in d_* modules need updating
New endpoints → Mobile API routes need adding
Changed auth requirements → Mobile interceptors may need updating
Changed error codes → Mobile error handling may need updating

When you detect a mobile-impacting change, explicitly state:

⚠️ Mobile Impact: This change affects the API contract. The Flutter app's d_<module>/ data module will need corresponding updates to [specific files].

How To Present Impact Analysis
After completing a code change, include a brief impact summary:
### Files Changed
- src/domain/note/domain/note.ts (added mood field)
- src/domain/note/infrastructure/entities/note.entity.ts (added mood column)
- src/domain/note/infrastructure/mappers/note.mapper.ts (map mood both directions)
- src/migrations/AddMoodToNote.ts (new migration)
- src/migrations/migrations.ts (registered migration)
- src/http-app/dto/note/create-note.dto.ts (added mood validation)
- src/http-app/dto/note/note-response.dto.ts (added mood to response)

### Verified No Impact
- Note summarize prompt (note-summarize.hbs) — does not use mood field currently
- Vision board queries — do not include note mood in responses

### ⚠️ Mobile Impact
- d_notes module: Note model needs `mood` field added
- d_notes API routes: create/update DTOs need mood parameter
The Golden Rule
If you change a file and don't check what depends on it, you are creating a bug. Every change must be traced through the dependency chain. When in doubt, search the codebase for imports/usages of the changed class, interface, or type.
